Phase 1: Foundation Metrics & Portfolio SetupObjective: Establish the structural boundaries for the system.Execution:Migrate all functional backend modules into the centralized /portfolio library directory.Append structural SDOA v5.0 Manifest blocks to all modules, including explicit runtime, operationalRole, and optimization parameters.Deploy Captain.service.js to manage uniform application bootstrap initialization sequences.  Phase 2: Automated Hooks & The RegistrarObjective: Implement live portfolio observation and dynamic registration.Execution:Code the Registrar.service.js module using a file-watcher library (e.g., chokidar inside Node.js) to monitor the /portfolio folder path.Build internal dependency maps using incoming manifest metrics, completely replacing manual configuration entries or switch arrays with automated routing tables.  Wire up Conductor.service.js to track active event interactions, resolving logical paths dynamically via the core EventBus infrastructure.  Phase 3: Parallel Runtimes & In-Memory LinkingObjective: Establish immediate polyglot bridging without external serialization overhead.Execution:Deploy AssemblyLine.service.js to capture and instantiate compiled WebAssembly files using WebAssembly.instantiate() parameters.  Structure direct data-sharing capabilities by mapping SharedArrayBuffer spaces and typed array variables (e.g., Float64Array) right to target binary heaps.  Configure fallback sub-process channels inside the Router to route tasks to non-Wasm elements (such as native Python executions or local COBOL integrations) via standard streams (stdout/stdin).Phase 4: The Autonomous Mutation LoopObjective: Wire up self-healing code adjustment cycles.Execution:Construct Coach.workflow.py to watch for processing bottlenecks or failing test runner scenarios. When an execution anomaly is isolated, the workflow packages the error metrics and constructs optimization paths via an LLM processing connector.Code ProbationOfficer.workflow.rs to secure the compilation loop. Every script generated by the Coach must pass through the ProbationOfficer sandbox engine to check for malicious logic or code structural failures.If validation succeeds, the new module is saved with a patch version bump, and the Registrar updates system paths to point to the upgraded asset.  Phase 5: Production Flight & OptimizationObjective: Enforce performance boundaries and system longevity.Execution:Initialize the Triage.workflow.js balancer. Allow the engine to review continuous execution telemetry (like tracking processing latencies or calculating memory usage footprints) to choose the best available module for every request.Activate structural volume assertions. Ensure that files that cross configured bounds (such as an engine module hitting over 150 lines) are automatically flagged for decomposition by the evolutionary loop.  6. SDOA v5.0 Anti-Patterns (The Prohibited List)To protect system integrity, the ProbationOfficer sandboxing engine will instantly reject any asset that introduces any of the following technical behaviors:Prohibited Pattern 1: Hardcoded Cross-Language Internal ImportsViolation: A Python script attempting to directly parse a JavaScript file, or a C++ engine executing shell scripts to reach a sibling module.Correction: All interactions must travel through the declarative manifest layer via explicit requires arrays managed by the core Registrar.  Prohibited Pattern 2: Global Namespace State PollutionViolation: Using assignments like window.appState = ... or relying on globally accessible system variables to communicate across processing boundaries.  Correction: State storage metrics must pass explicitly through the standard StateStore interface methods (.get(), .set(), .watch()).  Prohibited Pattern 3: Monolithic Implementation GrowthViolation: Letting architectural components grow past structured file constraints (e.g., a primitive moving past 150 lines, or a workflow extending beyond 200 lines).  Correction: Large modules must be decomposed into sub-features or distinct adapters to maintain clarity for both human reviewers and automated processing engines.  Prohibited Pattern 4: Manual Exception Interception HacksViolation: Deploying unmanaged time delays (setTimeout) or configuring untracked background catch containers to intercept processing failures.  Correction: Life cycles must be governed using standard asynchronous methods, passing error arrays directly up to the Captain for state resolution.  